    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fab40d01-6a17-5e12-835f-851b2c004a35",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Express the following as a constant multiple of a single trigonometric ratio.\n\n$$\n6\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$. Recall $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6\\sin x\\cos x &=3\\left[2\\sin x\\cos x\\right] \\\\ &=3\\sin(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$ and double the whole angle: $2x=\\frac{4\\pi}{9}$. The result is\n\n$$\n3\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}\\right) .\n$$",
