      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{7}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{7}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{7}\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=\\frac{3\\pi}{7}$. Recall $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} &=2\\frac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=2\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=2\\tan(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is nonzero, and $\\tan(2x)=\\sin(2x)/\\cos(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{3\\pi}{7}$ and double the whole angle: $2x=\\frac{6\\pi}{7}$. The result is\n\n$$\n2\\tan\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{7}\\right) .\n$$",
