      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{11}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{11}\\right)}{2\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{11}\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=\\frac{\\pi}{11}$. Recall $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{-\\sin x\\cos x}{2\\cos(2x)} &=-\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=-\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=-\\frac{1}{4}\\tan(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is nonzero, and $\\tan(2x)=\\sin(2x)/\\cos(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{11}$ and double the whole angle: $2x=\\frac{2\\pi}{11}$. The result is\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}\\tan\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{11}\\right) .\n$$",
